necessary permissions.

35
00:02:58.525 --> 00:03:05.025
'Potential Harm' with checkmarks for privacy,
fairness, and transparency.

36
00:03:05.025 --> 00:03:12.083
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

37
00:03:12.083 --> 00:03:13.259
the goalposts.

38
00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:19.993
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

39
00:03:19.993 --> 00:03:26.941
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

40
00:03:26.941 --> 00:03:29.025
throughout the analysis.

41
00:03:29.138 --> 00:03:36.709
Apply Responsible Technical Communication to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

42
00:03:36.709 --> 00:03:37.791
isolated definition.

43
00:03:37.791 --> 00:03:43.509
Responsible technical communication
distinguishes claims from evidence.

44
00:03:43.509 --> 00:03:51.928
Consider this worked case: Students structure
their proposal to clearly separate their novel

45
00:03:51.928 --> 00:03:55.166
hypothesis from established literature
findings.

46
00:03:55.166 --> 00:04:00.681
'Evidence' (prior research citations), with
arrows linking them.

47
00:04:00.681 --> 00:04:07.918
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

48
00:04:07.918 --> 00:04:08.400
step.

49
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.431
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

50
00:04:13.431 --> 00:04:20.330
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

51
00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:22.400
throughout the analysis.

52
00:04:22.514 --> 00:04:29.448
Examine the tradeoffs in Results and
Limitations, including what improves and what

53
00:04:29.448 --> 00:04:31.182
becomes more difficult.

54
00:04:31.182 --> 00:04:37.399
Results should be reported accurately rather
than selected to create a misleading

55
00:04:37.399 --> 00:04:37.917
conclusion.

56
00:04:37.917 --> 00:04:44.428
Consider this worked case: Students draft a
subsection explicitly stating potential biases

57
00:04:44.428 --> 00:04:49.854
in their dataset and how they will validate
their findings.

58
00:04:49.854 --> 00:04:56.729
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

59
00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:04.292
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

60
00:05:04.394 --> 00:05:12.174
Stress-test the reasoning around Research
Integrity and Plagiarism with an edge case and

61
00:05:12.174 --> 00:05:15.765
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

62
00:05:15.765 --> 00:05:23.678
Authors should preserve research integrity and
avoid plagiarism, fabrication, and

63
00:05:23.678 --> 00:05:24.469
falsification.

64
00:05:24.469 --> 00:05:30.771
Consider this worked case: Students use
citation management tools to track all

65
00:05:30.771 --> 00:05:36.547
references and verify that all code and data
are properly attributed.

66
00:05:36.547 --> 00:05:42.859
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

67
00:05:42.859 --> 00:05:50.109
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

68
00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:57.871
Use Supervisor Deliverables and Planning to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

69
00:05:57.871 --> 00:06:00.425
causes, and corrective action.

70
00:06:00.425 --> 00:06:09.601
Additional deliverables set by the advisor are
also required (for example, source code,

71
00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:12.425
bibliographies, notes, presentations, etc.

72
00:06:12.425 --> 00:06:19.795
Consider this worked case: Students create a
project management tracker that logs weekly

73
00:06:19.795 --> 00:06:24.331
milestones for coding, literature review, and
draft presentations.

74
00:06:24.331 --> 00:06:32.078
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

75
00:06:32.078 --> 00:06:33.738
smallest plausible cause.

76
00:06:33.738 --> 00:06:41.472
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

77
00:06:41.594 --> 00:06:48.134
Connect Evaluation Criteria and Quality
Standards to the earlier ideas and explain how

78
00:06:48.134 --> 00:06:51.153
the pieces work as one system.

79
00:06:51.153 --> 00:06:58.122
Professionals should strive to achieve high
quality in both processes and products.

80
00:06:58.122 --> 00:07:06.263
Consider this worked case: Students apply a
rubric that scores their proposal on clarity,

81
00:07:06.263 --> 00:07:11.497
methodological rigor, ethical compliance, and
alignment with advisor goals.

82
00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:21.126
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

83
00:07:21.126 --> 00:07:22.731
across representations.

84
00:07:22.731 --> 00:07:29.997
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

85
00:07:30.122 --> 00:07:36.768
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

86
00:07:36.768 --> 00:07:37.243
analysis.

87
00:07:37.243 --> 00:07:44.790
At the end of the semester, a thesis proposal
must be submitted for approval to the advisor.

88
00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:54.117
Consider this worked case: Students run a
final review against a checklist: problem

89
00:07:54.117 --> 00:07:59.857
statement, literature review, reproducible
methods, ethical considerations, citations,

90
00:07:59.857 --> 00:08:02.009
